<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Félix Éboué</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1884–1944</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Félix Éboué was a French colonial administrator born in French Guiana, the first Black governor of a French colony. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> he declared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1940, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> followed. Appointed governor-general, he promoted policies for African elites that influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> of 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-eboue.jpg" alt="Félix Éboué" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author (Keystone-France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%A9lix_%C3%89bou%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
